{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-107545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=5$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+29x-45=0,$ find the remaining solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-107545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+29x-45.$ Since $x=5$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{5} & 1 & -9 & 29 & -45 \\\\  &  & 5 & -20 & 45 \\\\  & 1 & -4 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x-5)({x}^{2}-4x+9).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-4x+9=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=5,\\,2\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-107545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "23cda73264c358617cb7911c464ce4dd25cfae294760e1ed7c483f800fe42de1"} {"question" "q-107545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-107545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}"] ["math" "1\\pm \\text{i}"] ["math" "2\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}"] ["math" "3\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "5\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-107545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $36{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+4x+1=0,$ find the remaining solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)=36{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+4x+1.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $36$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-9$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $36$ | $0$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(36{x}^{2}+4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $36{x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 36{x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "23cda73264c358617cb7911c464ce4dd25cfae294760e1ed7c483f800fe42de1"} {"question" "q-104500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "1\\pm \\frac{1}{9}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\pm 3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\pm 9\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-317978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25=0,$ find the remaining solutions.\n\n$x=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-317978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $1$ | $100$ | $25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $4$ | $0$ | $100$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}+100).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $4{x}^{2}+100=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =-100 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(5\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm 5\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-317978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "23cda73264c358617cb7911c464ce4dd25cfae294760e1ed7c483f800fe42de1"} {"question" "q-317978" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-317978" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5i"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-17409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=-10$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-24x+160=0,$ find the remaining solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-17409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-24x+160.$ Since $x=-10$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+10)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+10)$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{-10} & 1 & 6 & -24 & 160 \\\\  &  & -10 & 40 & -160 \\\\  & 1 & -4 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+10)({x}^{2}-4x+16).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-4x+16=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-12 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-10,\\,2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-17409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "23cda73264c358617cb7911c464ce4dd25cfae294760e1ed7c483f800fe42de1"} {"question" "q-17409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-17409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "2\\pm 3\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}"] ["math" "3\\pm 2\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}"] ["math" "3\\pm 3\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "4\\pm 4\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-17409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a solution to the equation $3{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+8x-2=0,$ find the remaining solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)=9{x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+24x-6.$ Since $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{\\frac{1}{3}} & 3 & -7 & 8 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\  & 3 & -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{3})(3{x}^{2}-6x+6).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $3{x}^{2}-6x+6=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x+6 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-2x+2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =(\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,1\\pm \\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "23cda73264c358617cb7911c464ce4dd25cfae294760e1ed7c483f800fe42de1"} {"question" "q-104497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1\\pm \\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "1\\pm \\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\pm \\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1\\pm \\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-101758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find all of the solutions to $2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8=0$ given that $x=2-2\\text{i}$ is a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-101758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=2-2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{2-2\\text{i}}=2+2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(2-2\\text{i}))(x-(2+2\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(2-2\\text{i}))(x-(2+2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(2-2\\text{i}+2+2\\text{i})x+(2-2\\text{i})(2+2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+({2}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-4x+8$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-4x+8\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+16x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+4x-8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,{x}^{2}+4x-8}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+8)(2x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=\\frac{1}{2},2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-101758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "23cda73264c358617cb7911c464ce4dd25cfae294760e1ed7c483f800fe42de1"} {"question" "q-101758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-101758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-\\frac{1}{4},2\\pm 2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{2},2-2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{2},2\\pm 2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{8},2\\pm 2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{8},2-2\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-101758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{1}{2},2\\pm 2\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20=0$ given that $x=3-\\text{i}$ is a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=3-\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{3-\\text{i}}=3+\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(3-\\text{i}+(3+\\text{i)})x+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+({3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-6x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+2 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-6x+10\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}-12x+20 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}-12x+20}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6x+10)(x+2).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "23cda73264c358617cb7911c464ce4dd25cfae294760e1ed7c483f800fe42de1"} {"question" "q-104514" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104514" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-2,0,3-\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}"] ["math" "x=0,3-\\text{i},1+3\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=2,3\\pm \\text{i}"] ["math" "x=\\pm 2,3-\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104514" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-107548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+9x-9=0$ given that $x=-3\\text{i}$ is a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-107548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+9x-9.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-3\\text{i}}=3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}+(3\\text{i})(-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+9$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+9\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+9x-9\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+9x}_{―} \\\\  & -{x}^{2}\\,\\,-9 \\\\  & \\,{-\\,{x}^{2}\\,\\,-9}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+9)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1,\\pm 3\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-107548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "23cda73264c358617cb7911c464ce4dd25cfae294760e1ed7c483f800fe42de1"} {"question" "q-107548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-107548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=1,2,3\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=1,3,3\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=1,\\pm 3\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=2,\\pm 3\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=3,\\pm 3\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-107548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=1,\\pm 3\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116=0$ given that $x=-5+2\\text{i}$ is a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116.$\n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-5+2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-5+2\\text{i}}=-5-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(-5+2\\text{i}))(x-(-5-2\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-5+2\\text{i}))(x-(-5-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(-5+2\\text{i}+(-5-2\\text{i)})x+(-5+2\\text{i})(-5-2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+((-5)^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+29 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+10x+29$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+10x+29\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}+29x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,-4{x}^{2}+40x-116 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{-4{x}^{2}+40x-116}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+10x+29)(x-4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "23cda73264c358617cb7911c464ce4dd25cfae294760e1ed7c483f800fe42de1"} {"question" "q-104516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=-4,0,-5+2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=0,4,-5+2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=\\pm 4,-5+2\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-104516" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-317986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine all the solutions to ${x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25=0$ given that $x=5\\text{i}$ is a solution. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-317986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=5\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{5\\text{i}}=-5\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, $(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i}))$ is a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}+(5\\text{i})(-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+25$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+25\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+25x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}\\,-25 \\\\  & \\,\\,{\\,-{x}^{2}\\,\\,-25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+25)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1$ and $x=\\pm \\,5\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-317986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "23cda73264c358617cb7911c464ce4dd25cfae294760e1ed7c483f800fe42de1"} {"question" "q-317986" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-317986" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-317986" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "q-317986" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5i"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/state.json" "file_sha256" "978deabe6126718ced20aefabe93fe7f993074800367c5003db7d03cfa4e6fc9"} {"question" "e-5794" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x=-2$ is a solution to the equation $x^3+8 = 0,$ find the remaining solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/example-5794.json" "file_sha256" "3769a13244a7a77559f04b4088e708dffaa47dcc8b23a12b4f3a723768ce060d"} {"question" "e-5794" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x) = x^3+8.$ Since $x=-2$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/assets/a92f1e322fc91c8388e7969117aca8e616625128d82ee12c88ce108cb6b2637e.png)\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x+2)(x^2-2x+4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $x^2-2x+4 = 0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-2, \\, 1\\pm \\sqrt3\\textrm{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/example-5794.json" "file_sha256" "3769a13244a7a77559f04b4088e708dffaa47dcc8b23a12b4f3a723768ce060d"} {"question" "e-8800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find all of the solutions to $x^3-3x^2+12x-10 = 0$ given that $x=1+3\\text{i}$ is a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/example-8800.json" "file_sha256" "9b74f58f4eb417fa35aa5ee9e29a98c976a0d835ad6aa20869c37017023e308a"} {"question" "e-8800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we let $f(x) = x^3-3x^2+12x-10.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and $x_1=1+3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{1+3\\textrm{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$\\big(x-(1+3\\text{i})\\big)\\big(x-(1-3\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(1+3\\text{i}+1-3\\text{i})x+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+({1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2 - 2x + 10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+10\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+12x-10\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+2x-10 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,-\\,{x}^{2}+2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2 - 2x + 10)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1, 1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/example-8800.json" "file_sha256" "9b74f58f4eb417fa35aa5ee9e29a98c976a0d835ad6aa20869c37017023e308a"}] "usage" {"e-8800" [] "q-101758" [] "q-104500" [] "e-5794" []} "basis" 2146 "retractions" [[17592186176128 :question/problem "Given that $x =-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $36x^{3} + 9x^{2} + 4x + 1 = 0$, find the remaining solutions."] [17592186176098 :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to $2x^{3} - 9x^{2} + 20x - 8 = 0$ given that $x = 2 - 2\\textrm{i}$ is a solution."] [17592186175708 :question/problem "Given that $x=-2$ is a solution to the equation $x^3+8 = 0$, find the remaining solutions."] [17592186175708 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^3+8\n$$\n\nSince $x=-2$ is a root of $f(x)$, then according to the factor theorem $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$.\n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}8; - 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x+2)(x^2-2x+4)\n$$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve\n\n$$\nx^2-2x+4 = 0\n$$\n\nby completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= -3 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= (\\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i})^{2} \\\\\nx - 1 &= ± \\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i} \\\\\nx &= 1 ± \\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are\n\n$$\nx=-2, \\, 1\\pm \\sqrt3\\textrm{i}\n$$"] [17592186175734 :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^3-3x^2+12x-10\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and\n\n$$\nx_1=1+3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nis a root of $f(x)$, the complex conjugate\n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{1+3\\textrm{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x)$.\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem,\n$\\big(x-(1+3\\text{i})\\big)\\big(x-(1-3\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x)$. Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - (1 + 3\\textrm{i}))(x - (1 - 3\\textrm{i})) &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - (1 + 3\\textrm{i} + 1 - 3\\textrm{i})x + (1 + 3\\textrm{i})(1 - 3\\textrm{i}) &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + (1^{2} - (3\\textrm{i})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 2x + 10 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2 - 2x + 10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\nx^{2} - 2x + 10x - 1; x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 12x - 10; x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 2x^{2} + 10x_(―); x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 0x^{2} + 02x - 10; x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3}- 0x^{2} + 02x - 10_(―); x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 12x - 10\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x^2 - 2x + 10)(x-1)\n$$\n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\nx - 1 = 0⟹x = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are\n\n$$\nx=1, 1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186173251 17592186173252 17592186173253 17592186173254 17592186173255 17592186173431 17592186173432 17592186173433 17592186173434 17592186173435] "immutable_fields" [[17592186173250 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173250 :answer-field/id] [17592186173250 :answer-field/key] [17592186173250 :answer-field/type] [17592186173430 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173430 :answer-field/id] [17592186173430 :answer-field/key] [17592186173430 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
